				<h1>T3 Code alternative: Zoes vs T3 Code</h1>
				<div class="post-meta">By Minh Ngo · Published <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time> · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time></div>
				<p class="lede">T3 Code is a free, MIT-licensed control plane for the coding agents you already use, with desktop, web and phone clients. Zoes is also free and MIT licensed. Its window is native Rust on GPUI, and its daemon, pocketd, keeps local sessions running after you close the window. It needs no account and promises no premium tier, ever.</p>
				<div class="callout">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Disclosure</span>The Zoes team wrote this. T3 Code facts come from <a href="https://t3.codes/">t3.codes</a>, its <a href="https://t3.codes/download">download page</a>, and the README and user docs in <a href="https://github.com/pingdotgg/t3code">pingdotgg/t3code</a>, checked on 18 November 2026. Both projects ship often, so check the dates. If something here is out of date, <a href="https://github.com/mingo023/anywhere/issues">open an issue</a>.</p>
				</div>

				<h2 id="t3-code">T3 Code in one paragraph</h2>
				<p>T3 Code is built by Theo and the T3 Tools team, in the <code>pingdotgg/t3code</code> repository. Its site calls it "The open-source control plane for coding agents" and goes on: "Orchestrate Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity, OpenCode, Cursor, and Grok from one surface. Bring your own subscription. Fork the whole thing." The README puts it this way: "T3 Code runs the coding agents on your machine and gives them one fast interface. Start a thread at your desk, then approve the next step from your phone." Its FAQ is clear that it sits on top of your agents: "It is not a harness, and it does not work without one." One server feeds desktop, web, and phone clients that show the same threads. The app is MIT licensed, and the FAQ says "There's no way to pay us to use T3 Code. You bring your own sub."</p>

				<h2 id="anywhere">Zoes in one paragraph</h2>
				<p>Zoes is a desktop app for watching and driving many coding agents at once. The window is Rust on GPUI. A Go background service, pocketd, owns the terminals and sessions. It runs the agent CLIs you already installed and signed in to, puts each session under a project and a git worktree, tells you which one needs you, and keeps the diff and the pull request beside the session. The desktop app, daemon, iPhone app, and push relay are all in one MIT-licensed repository.</p>

