				<h2 id="where-anywhere-is-different">Where Zoes is different</h2>
				<p>Both apps are free and MIT licensed, and both run the agent CLIs you already pay for. The differences are in how they are built and what they promise.</p>

				<h3>A native window on GPUI</h3>
				<p>Zoes's window is Rust on <a href="https://www.gpui.rs">GPUI</a>, and the site's architecture line is "No Electron. No Tauri. Native all the way down." T3 Code's desktop app is Electron, written in TypeScript, per the <code>package.json</code> in its public repository. That is a toolkit choice, not a verdict: we don't publish benchmarks against other apps. If the window matters to you, open your largest repository in each and watch it while a few agents work. Why we went native is in <a href="/blog/native-mac-app-coding-agents-rust-gpui">Native, not Electron</a>.</p>

				<h3>Local sessions outlive the window, with nothing to set up</h3>
				<p>T3 Code's remote-access docs say: "Closing the desktop app's last window stops its local server." T3 Code does have a way to keep a host up without the window: <code>t3 up</code> or <code>t3 service</code>, which keep a background service running. In Zoes, that separation is the default. pocketd is the background service, it starts at login, and the window is only a client, so closing it doesn't stop an agent or a scheduled automation. On macOS the app also stays in the background by default. See <a href="/blog/coding-agents-keep-running-after-closing-window">Keep coding agents running after you close the window</a>.</p>

				<h3>No product account</h3>
				<p>There is no Zoes account for the desktop app, the daemon, or the CLI. The documented phone path pairs the iPhone app with the Mac over your own Tailscale network. T3 Code also works over LAN or Tailscale without T3 Connect; T3 Connect is the path that needs a T3 Connect account. See <a href="/docs/iphone">Pair an iPhone</a>.</p>

				<h3>A public promise of no premium tier, ever</h3>
				<p>Zoes's site says: "There is no account to make, no seat to buy and no feature held back for a paid plan. There never will be." Its pricing table lists the cloud tier and premium tier as "Never". T3 Code's app is free too, and T3 Connect is free "for now", with a machine cap. Its FAQ is candid about the trade-off: "We don't want to charge for this, but if the bill gets unacceptable we may have to change course. If you hit limits or have issues, you can always fork it, self host, or use Tailscale." Zoes sidesteps the question: the only service the project runs is an optional push relay, and the phone reaches the Mac over your own tailnet. More in <a href="/blog/open-source-agent-workspace-no-paid-tier">A free, open-source agent workspace with no paid tier</a>.</p>

				<h3>Automations, Board, and SSH hosts in the same app</h3>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="/docs/automations">Automations</a> start a prompt on chosen weekdays at a time, or every few minutes, and keep firing while the desktop app is closed.</li>
					<li>The <a href="/docs/board">Board</a> holds tickets across projects. A started ticket gets its own worktree and session.</li>
					<li><a href="/docs/hosts">SSH hosts</a> add a Mac, Linux, or Windows machine. Zoes installs pocketd there and runs that host's agents on it.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2 id="when-t3-code-may-fit-better">When T3 Code may fit better</h2>
				<div class="callout">
					<p><span class="callout-title">T3 Code is the better pick if you want</span></p>
					<ul>
						<li>Phone and browser clients you can use today: iOS on the App Store, Android on Google Play, and the web client at <code>app.t3.codes</code>. Zoes's iPhone app is documented but not yet public, and it has no Android or web client.</li>
						<li>Windows and Linux as first-class targets, with package managers (winget, Scoop, Arch, Nix, <code>.deb</code>) and an updater. Zoes's Linux and Windows builds are unsigned archives with no updater.</li>
						<li>A wider agent policy: a longer named list that includes Kiro and Muse Code, plus any ACP agent, with model switching mid-thread and handing a task from one agent to another, such as Claude to Codex. Zoes supports a fixed list of ten CLIs.</li>
						<li>A built-in remote, T3 Connect, that doesn't need Tailscale, with LAN and Tailscale also supported. Zoes's phone path needs Tailscale.</li>
						<li>One button to commit, push, and open a GitHub pull request with a generated title and body, including draft, stacked, and amended PRs.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<h2 id="using-both">Trying both</h2>
				<p>Neither app proxies your models, so switching costs little. Eight agents are on both named lists: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Grok Build, OpenCode, Pi, fx, and Antigravity. Both run the CLIs installed and signed in on your machine, so there is no new model account to set up.</p>
				<ol>
					<li>Install Zoes and add your repository as a project.</li>
					<li>Move per-worktree setup into <code>.zoes/config.json</code> or <code>.zoes/setup.sh</code>, so new worktrees install dependencies before the agent starts.</li>
					<li>Press <kbd>⌘⇧N</kbd> to start a session in a new worktree, from a branch or from a pull request number.</li>
					<li>Review in <strong>Changes</strong> and merge from the pull request card.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The step-by-step version is <a href="/blog/run-multiple-claude-code-agents-in-parallel">How to run multiple Claude Code agents in parallel</a>. For the wider field, see <a href="/blog/best-tools-run-multiple-coding-agents">the best tools to run multiple coding agents in parallel</a>, and for a macOS-only comparison, <a href="/blog/conductor-alternative">Zoes vs Conductor</a>.</p>

				<section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq">
					<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
					<h3>What is a good T3 Code alternative?</h3>
					<p>T3 Code is a free, MIT-licensed control plane for the coding agents you already use, with desktop, web and phone clients. Zoes is also free and MIT licensed. Its window is native Rust on GPUI, and its daemon, pocketd, keeps local sessions running after you close the window. It needs no account and promises no premium tier, ever.</p>
					<h3>Is T3 Code open source?</h3>
					<p>Yes. T3 Code is MIT licensed, its source is in the pingdotgg/t3code repository, and its FAQ says there is no way to pay to use it. Zoes is MIT licensed too, so open source is not what separates them.</p>
					<h3>Do agents keep running when I close the window?</h3>
					<p>In Zoes, yes, by default. pocketd starts at login and owns the terminals and sessions, so closing the window doesn't stop an agent. T3 Code's remote-access docs say closing the desktop app's last window stops its local server. To keep a T3 Code host available without the window, you run t3 up or t3 service, which keep a background service running.</p>
					<h3>Which agents do T3 Code and Zoes support?</h3>
					<p>T3 Code names Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode, Grok Build, Antigravity, Kiro, Pi, Muse Code and fx, and says it runs any ACP agent. Zoes runs a fixed list of ten CLIs: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Grok Build, OpenCode, Pi, omp, fx, Hermes Agent and Antigravity.</p>
					<h3>Do T3 Code and Zoes have phone apps?</h3>
					<p>T3 Code has an iOS app on the App Store, an Android app on Google Play, and a web client at app.t3.codes. Zoes's native iPhone and iPad app pairs with the Mac over Tailscale and is built and documented, but the site still lists it as coming soon. Zoes has no Android or web client.</p>
					<h3>Is T3 Connect free?</h3>
					<p>T3 Code's FAQ says T3 Connect is free too, for now. Each T3 Connect account can link up to 3 machines, and a linked paid T3 Chat account raises that limit. LAN pairing and Tailscale work without T3 Connect. Zoes has no hosted remote service: its phone app pairs over your own Tailscale network.</p>
				</section>
